// A function may reuse a module alias's name for a variable: the function is its own
// strong environment, and MEM-7 leaves a name free between strong environments. There
// `alias::fn(…)` still addresses the module, and `alias.name` reads the variable.
//
// The web interpreter (web/zymbol.js) once parsed `::` and `.` into the same node and
// resolved the object as an ordinary expression, so once a variable took the alias's
// name the module became unreachable and the call failed with "'.<name>' requires a
// named tuple" — an error naming the wrong operator and the wrong problem.
//
// This is not a contrived pattern: zyKlingonGalaxy imported `Duj` (ship) under a Klingon
// alias and then used that same name for the player's ship value, so every left/right
// move ran `duj = duj::bIj(duj, …)`. The game was unplayable in the browser because of it.
//
// At FILE level the same reuse is refused since 2026-09-26 (GLB-070): the file is the
// environment the `<#` lives in, and there a name designates one thing
// (reject/modules/05_file_variable_takes_an_alias_name.zy).

<# ./hlz/salida => s

// The alias resolves to the module.
>> "const antes:   " (s.LIMITE) ¶

prueba() {
    // Now let a plain variable of the function take the same name.
    s = 10
    >> "variable:      " s ¶

    // `::` still reaches the module, and the variable is unaffected by it.
    >> "slice:         " (s::cola("abcdef", 2)) ¶
    >> "variable otra vez: " s ¶

    // Reassigning through a call whose callee is the shadowed alias — the exact
    // shape the Klingon game used on every move.
    s = s::cola("XY9", 1)
    >> "reasignada:    " s ¶
}
prueba()

// Outside the function the name is the module again.
>> "const despues: " (s.LIMITE) ¶
